<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Florence</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Florence is a city in Tuscany, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and a leading centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. Byzantine émigré scholars contributed greatly to the revival of Greek learning there, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-florence">Council of Florence</a> (1438–1445) sought to reunite the Byzantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> churches in the last years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florence.jpg" alt="Florence" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giorgio Galeotti · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firenze_-_Piazzale_Michelangelo,_Firenze,_Italy_-_April_6,_2015_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…scientific heritage preserved in Byzantine libraries, transmitted to Italy by scholars such as Manuel Chrysoloras from 1397 and by émigrés after the empire's fall, helped shape the Italian Renaissance: the recovery of Plato and the Greek historians in <b>Florence</b>, Venice and Rome was substantially mediated by Byzantine scholarship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By the summer of 1402 <b>Florence</b> seemed isolated, but Gian Galeazzo died of plague in September of that year, and his state broke apart in the hands of his young sons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the republic’s last revival was crushed by imperial and papal forces in the siege of 1529–1530, the Emperor Charles V made Alessandro de’ Medici hereditary Duke of <b>Florence</b> in 1532.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome became the capital in 1871, after interim spells in Turin and, from 1865, <b>Florence</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ladislaus of Naples (r. 1386–1414) built one of the strongest armies in Italy with condottieri, occupied Rome in 1408 and threatened <b>Florence</b> before his sudden death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Florence</b> began as the Roman colony of Florentia on the Arno, traditionally founded in 59 BCE, and in the early Middle Ages it was a modest town of the March of Tuscany.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
